      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting point \\( A \\) and point \\( B \\) in a rectangular prism with dimensions 10 inches by 20 inches by 10 inches, we can use the 3-dimensional version of the Pythagorean theorem. The length of the diagonal \\( d \\) of a rectangular prism with side lengths \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}\n\\]\n\nHere, the side lengths of the rectangular prism are \\( a = 10 \\) inches, \\( b = 20 \\) inches, and \\( c = 10 \\) inches. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the squares of the side lengths:\n\n\\[\n10^2 = 100, \\quad 20^2 = 400, \\quad 10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together:\n\n\\[\n100 + 400 + 100 = 600\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of the sum:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{600}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\sqrt{600}\\) by factoring it into prime factors:\n\n\\[\n600 = 100 \\times 6 = 10^2 \\times 6\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{10^2 \\times 6} = 10\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal connecting point \\( A \\) and point \\( B \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\n\\]",
