      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for the length of the diagonal connecting points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) in the rectangular prism, we can use the 3D Pythagorean theorem. The theorem states that for a rectangular prism with side lengths \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\), the length of the diagonal \\( d \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven the dimensions of the rectangular prism:\n- Length (\\( a \\)) = 20 inches\n- Width (\\( b \\)) = 10 inches\n- Height (\\( c \\)) = 10 inches\n\nWe can substitute \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) into the formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{20^2 + 10^2 + 10^2}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the squares of each dimension:\n\n\\[\n20^2 = 400\n\\]\n\\[\n10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\\[\n10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nNext, we sum these squared values:\n\n\\[\n400 + 100 + 100 = 600\n\\]\n\nNow, we take the square root of the sum:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{600}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\sqrt{600}\\), we factorize 600:\n\n\\[\n600 = 6 \\times 100 = 6 \\times 10^2\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{6 \\times 10^2} = \\sqrt{6} \\times \\sqrt{10^2} = \\sqrt{6} \\times 10 = 10\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal connecting points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\n\\]",
