      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal of the rectangular prism, we will use the 3-dimensional version of the Pythagorean theorem. Let's denote the dimensions of the rectangular prism as follows:\n- Length \\( l = 20 \\) inches\n- Width \\( w = 10 \\) inches\n- Height \\( h = 10 \\) inches\n\nThe formula for the length of the diagonal \\( d \\) of a rectangular prism is given by:\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2} \\]\n\nSubstituting the given dimensions into the formula, we get:\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{20^2 + 10^2 + 10^2} \\]\n\nNow, we calculate each term inside the square root:\n\\[ 20^2 = 400 \\]\n\\[ 10^2 = 100 \\]\n\\[ 10^2 = 100 \\]\n\nAdding these values together:\n\\[ 400 + 100 + 100 = 600 \\]\n\nSo, the length of the diagonal is:\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{600} \\]\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\sqrt{600}\\) by factoring it into prime factors:\n\\[ 600 = 4 \\times 150 = 4 \\times 3 \\times 50 = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 25 = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 5^2 = 2^3 \\times 3 \\times 5^2 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ \\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{2^3 \\times 3 \\times 5^2} = \\sqrt{2^2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2} = \\sqrt{2^2} \\times \\sqrt{3} \\times \\sqrt{5^2} = 2 \\times \\sqrt{3} \\times 5 = 10\\sqrt{3} \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal is:\n\\[ \\boxed{10\\sqrt{3}} \\]",
